      <circle cx="110" cy="60" r="26"/>
      <circle cx="110" cy="60" r="13" fill="#1a282a" />
    </g></svg>
<p class="cormorant center">the obedient have been answered. the rest are now obliged to read.</p>
<hr>
<p>Three visitors entered the <a href="/mill/">mill</a> on the same morning, in 1971. Only two of them, when the count was taken, could be accounted for. The <span class="tilt-l">third</span> is, the house assures us, in the <a href="/oubliette/">oubliette</a>, and is well, and prefers not to be disturbed.</p>
<h2>ii. on the colour of the walls</h2>
<p class="huge tilt-3 smear">wait &nbsp; <span class="flicker">wait</span></p>
<h2>i. on the colour of the walls</h2><p><span class="h">O</span>ften the visitors look only at what shines. They are escorted, gently, back to the threshold, and the house arranges for them to remember nothing of the visit.</p><p><span class="h">S</span>ometimes a word holds another word inside it, the way a coffin holds a coffin, the way a room holds a room.</p><p><span class="h">S</span>elect what you cannot see. This is the instruction the house gives most often, and which the visitors most often decline.</p><p><span class="h">U</span>nder every floor is a floor. Under that floor is a name. Under the name is the person the name once belonged to.</p><p><span class="h">A</span>sk the room what it is, and the room, on a good day, will tell you. Ask politely. Ask in lower case.</p><p><span class="h">R</span>ooms are not buildings. Rooms are sentences. You are reading one now.</p><p><span class="h">Y</span>ou are nearly through. The next chamber is named for what we keep when there is nothing else left.</p>
<p class="type">The <a href="/library/">library</a> <span class="smear">keeps</span> a list. The list is of every visitor who has ever asked, on entering, where the <a href="/dairy/">dairy</a> is. The list is bound; the list is long; the list is the <a href="/library/">library</a>, written down.</p>
<p>The <a href="/gallery/">gallery</a> is named for a person. The person is not, the house notes, anyone the visitor will recognise. The <a href="/study/">study</a> keeps the relevant correspondence, which the visitor is not, in any case, encouraged to read.</p>
<p class="cormorant">If you stand in the <a href="/attic/">attic</a> and whisper the name of the <a href="/corridor/">corridor</a>, the <a href="/corridor/">corridor</a> will, very faintly, whisper back. It will not, on any occasion, whisper your name.</p>
<p class="tilt-l">The wallpaper in the <a href="/baptistery/">baptistery</a> is, on inspection, a repeating pattern of the <a href="/atelier/">atelier</a>. On closer inspection it is the same <a href="/atelier/">atelier</a>, every time. On closer inspection still it is the visitor.</p>
<p class="tilt-r">The <a href="/guardroom/">guardroom</a> was, in an <span class="tilt-r">earlier</span> century, the <a href="/smithy/">smithy</a>. The <a href="/smithy/">smithy</a> was, before that, the <a href="/scullery/">scullery</a>. The house considers these distinctions, on its better days, ornamental.</p>
<p class="wide">The <a href="/drawing/">drawing</a> keeps a list. The list is of every visitor who has ever asked, on entering, where the <a href="/oubliette/">oubliette</a> is. The list is bound; the list is long; the <span class="breathe">list</span> is the <a href="/drawing/">drawing</a>, written down.</p>
<p class="tight">The <a href="/apse/">apse</a> smells, faintly, of the <a href="/conservatory/">conservatory</a>. This is not, the house insists, intentional. It is, however, persistent, and the visitors have learned, in time, to find it reassuring.</p>
<p>You are looking for a name. The name is a place. The name is hidden in this room, in a colour the walls were not, on the whole, painted in. When you have read the walls correctly, the name will appear among the <a href="/ossuary/">ossuary</a> you have already been shown, and you will know which door to take.</p>
<hr>
